    "winner": "01__19559842-SUPPLEMENTAL-RESPONSE-ASSERTIVE-10202026.tex",
    "collective_statement_most_likely_to_succeed": "The panel unanimously selects Candidate #1 (noting that all candidates presented identical, highly optimized text) as the most likely to succeed. The document executes a brilliant strategic mutation by overriding the rigid 'Knapsack' fee directive (which assumed only $520.00 in available credits) and instead leveraging the $960.00 erroneous excess claims payment (Fee Code 3201) identified in the docket XML. By aggregating the $520.00 and $960.00 into a total pool of $1,480.00 in unapplied credits, the Applicant successfully directs the Office to apply $970.00 to fully satisfy the Small Entity base fees without requiring any fresh cash remittance, while properly requesting the $510.00 surplus be deposited into their USPTO Deposit Account under 37 CFR 1.25(b). Furthermore, the document executes the highly rewarded preemptive strike of filing a Substitute Specification with a 37 CFR 1.125(b) No New Matter Certification to excise embedded drawings and segregate claims/abstracts, perfectly curing all formal defects.",
    "unlikely_to_succeed_reasons": null,
    "ats_alignment_assessment": "The candidate aligns perfectly with the core statutory mandates of the ATS brief while intelligently deviating from the automated Path B Waterfall Directive. Instead of blindly following the heuristic that suggested paying $480.00 in fresh cash, the candidate adopted the Attorney Summary's recommendation to petition for the reallocation of the $960.00 overpayment under 37 CFR 1.26 and 1.22(b). This demonstrates superior prosecution strategy.",
